---
layout: null
sitemap: false
---
{%- comment -%}
  THE SITEMAP.

  This file replaces the one jekyll-sitemap generates. The plugin skips its own
  generator whenever a sitemap.xml already exists in the source, so this is the
  file that ships - and it is here because the plugin's default emits a bare
  <loc> per page and nothing else. In particular it only writes <lastmod> from
  `last_modified_at`, never from `date`, so a page with only a date in its front
  matter silently gets no freshness signal at all.

  Every page drives its own row from front matter:
    last_modified_at:  overrides date. Use it when the copy changes.
    date:              the fallback, and what most pages carry.
    changefreq:        defaults to monthly.
    priority:          defaults to 0.7.
    sitemap: false     keeps a page out entirely.

  /404.html is excluded explicitly as well as by its own `sitemap: false` - an
  error page has no business in a sitemap, and on a four-page site one junk URL
  would be a quarter of the index.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign pages = site.html_pages | where_exp:'p','p.sitemap != false' | where_exp:'p','p.url != "/404.html"' | sort: 'url' -%}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for p in pages %}
  <url>
    <loc>{{ p.url | replace:'/index.html','/' | absolute_url | xml_escape }}</loc>
{%- assign lastmod = p.last_modified_at | default: p.date -%}
{%- if lastmod %}
    <lastmod>{{ lastmod | date_to_xmlschema }}</lastmod>
{%- endif %}
    <changefreq>{{ p.changefreq | default: 'monthly' }}</changefreq>
    <priority>{{ p.priority | default: '0.7' }}</priority>
  </url>
{%- endfor %}
</urlset>
